# `integ::quadra_scalar`

Integrates an ordinary `double(double)` function over an interval by composite Simpson.

## Signature

```cpp
integ::QuadraScalarResult integ::quadra_scalar(const integ::ScalarIntegrandFn &fn, double a, double b, const integ::QuadraScalarOptions &options = {});
```

## What It Does

For a function that is plain number-in, number-out arithmetic and has nothing to do with `Series`, such as a density evaluated by a library routine. It evaluates `fn` at `nodes + 1` equally spaced points and applies composite Simpson's rule; with `adaptive` on it doubles the nodes until the change between two levels is within `tolerance` (relative for a large value), up to `max_refinements` times. `b ≤ a` returns zero. Because it calls `fn` one point at a time it is slower than [`quadra`](quadra.md) when the integrand can be written on a `Series`; use it only when it cannot.

## Parameters

`const integ::ScalarIntegrandFn &fn`
: `double -> double`.

`double a, double b`
: The interval; `b ≤ a` returns zero.

`const integ::QuadraScalarOptions &options`
: Node count and adaptivity.

## Returns

A [`QuadraScalarResult`](QuadraScalarResult.md).

## Errors

- A missing function, or fewer than two nodes, throws.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A plain function. `∫₀^π sin x dx = 2`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const integ::QuadraScalarResult r = integ::quadra_scalar([](double t) { return std::sin(t); }, 0.0, 3.14159265358979323846);
  out.writeln("value =", r.value, ", evaluations =", r.evaluations, ", converged =", r.converged);
}
```

### Example 2

Adaptive refinement. A function with a sharp feature, `1/(1 + 400 (x − 0.3)²)`, needs more nodes than the default 256; the adaptive run adds them until the estimate settles.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  integ::QuadraScalarOptions options;
  options.adaptive = true;
  options.nodes = 64;
  const integ::QuadraScalarResult r = integ::quadra_scalar([](double t) { return 1.0 / (1.0 + 400.0 * (t - 0.3) * (t - 0.3)); }, 0.0, 1.0, options);
  out.writeln("value =", r.value, ", error estimate =", r.error_estimate, ", evaluations =", r.evaluations, ", converged =", r.converged);
}
```

### Example 3

A function that wraps library code. The normal distribution function from its density: the probability below 1.96 starting from −8 is `0.975`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const auto density = [](double t) { return std::exp(-0.5 * t * t) / std::sqrt(2.0 * 3.14159265358979323846); };
  const integ::QuadraScalarResult r = integ::quadra_scalar(density, -8.0, 1.96);
  out.writeln("P(Z < 1.96) =", r.value);
}
```

